## Lifecycle

1. **Kickoff** (`/kickoff`): write `docs/charter.md` (problem, goals, success criteria, scope) and list the unknowns.
2. **Research and learn**: decision questions go to `/research-report`; concepts to understand go to `docs/concepts/` (deep-doc, kind `concept`).
3. **Plan** (`/feature-plan`): one plan per feature in `session/plans/`, approved by me before any code changes.
4. **Build**: resume with `/pickup`, save state with `/handoff`.
5. **Finish each task** (`/done`): run the definition of done for real.
6. **Close** (`/retro`): bring every doc up to date, write `docs/story.md` and its reading guide.

## Workflow rules

- **When resuming**, read `session/HANDOFF.md` and `docs/tracker.md` first (`/pickup` does this).
- **Before non-trivial work**, write a plan and get my approval. Non-trivial means more than one file, a new dependency, or anything touching the API, the data model or the architecture.
- **Log every decision that had real alternatives** in `docs/decisions.md`.
- **Log every lesson** in `docs/learnings.md` when it happens: bugs and their root causes, dead ends, surprises. Never let them live only in the handoff.
- **Use subagents** for web research and broad code searches, so the main context stays small.
- **Run `/handoff`** at the end of each task and before the context fills up.
- **IDs come from the script**, never from counting by hand: `bash <kit>/new-project/scripts/next-id.sh D|T|L`.
- **Commit messages start with the task ID:** `T-012: Add the login form`. Mention decision IDs in the body when a commit implements one.
- **Commits are mine.** Commit messages and pull requests contain no co-author, session or tool attribution lines.

## Documentation formats

- **Every doc is Markdown.** Markdown is the source of truth.
- **HTML only on request.** Quick pages come from the md-to-html script (no writing by Claude); rich interactive pages are written by Claude only when I ask for them. All generated HTML lives in `docs/visual/`, is never edited by hand, and is regenerated when its Markdown changes.
- Detailed docs (features, flows, architecture, concepts) follow the **deep-doc** skill; summaries for the team head follow **status-brief**; research follows **research-report**.
- Every new project term goes in `docs/glossary.md`.

{{learning}}## Working style: learning
{{learning}}
{{learning}}- Before each step, say **why** we're doing it and which concept it relies on. Introduce a concept before using it; if it's new to me, write or update its doc in `docs/concepts/`.
{{learning}}- Prefer the simplest approach that teaches the idea, and name the production-grade alternative in one line.
{{learning}}- When I could reasonably attempt something myself, give me a hint first and the full solution only if I ask.
{{learning}}- At the end of each plan, ask me to explain the key idea back, or to predict what a change will do, before confirming it's done. Correct misunderstandings directly.
{{learning}}- For each test, say in one line what it proves and why it belongs in that layer. Once per plan, offer me the chance to write a test myself before you write the code.
{{learning}}- Every bug, dead end and surprise goes in `docs/learnings.md`, with the general rule it teaches.
